                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "There is / there are, some / any",
                    "explanation": "There is (there's) y there are significan hay. Usa there is con singular o incontables (there's red wine) y there are con plurales (there are some burgers).\n\nSome (algunos, algo de) va en frases afirmativas; any, en negativas y preguntas: Is there any wine?, There aren't any chips. Para pedir u ofrecer usamos some también en preguntas: Can I have some bread?\n\nPara pedir educadamente: Can I have…, please? o … for me, please.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "Singular / incontable",
                        "Plural"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Afirmativa",
                          "There's some wine.",
                          "There are some burgers."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Negativa",
                          "There isn't any wine.",
                          "There aren't any burgers."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Pregunta",
                          "Is there any wine?",
                          "Are there any burgers?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Respuesta corta",
                          "Yes, there is. / No, there isn't.",
                          "Yes, there are. / No, there aren't."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "There's a vegetable curry.",
                      "Are there any vegetarian dishes? — Yes, there are some.",
                      "Can I have a lemonade, please?",
                      "Some bread for the table, please!"
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "How much / how many · I'd like",
                    "explanation": "Hay nombres contables (a burger, two burgers) e incontables, que no tienen plural ni llevan a/an: water, bread, wine, money, rice.\n\nHow many…? pregunta por cantidades de cosas contables: How many burgers? How much…? pregunta por incontables: How much water? Y también por el precio: How much is it? — It's thirty-two pounds fifty.\n\nI'd like (= I would like) es la forma educada de pedir algo: I'd like a glass of wine, please. Va seguido de un nombre o de to + verbo: I'd like to pay, please. Pregunta educada: Would you like some bread?",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "Contables",
                        "Incontables"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Ejemplos",
                          "a burger, two chips, three drinks",
                          "water, bread, wine, money"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Pregunta",
                          "How many burgers are there?",
                          "How much water is there?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Precio",
                          "How much are the burgers?",
                          "How much is the wine?"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "How much is a glass of red wine? — Five pounds.",
                      "How many people are there? — Three.",
                      "I'd like the vegetable curry, please.",
                      "Would you like some bread? — Yes, please."
                    ]
                  }
